Work, kinetic energy, potential and mechanical power (@mech-work-energy: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Engineering-Dynamics-MIT-2-003SC.lingua#r_37MSK6EJHRV95GXWA23P4ZJPZ3", #source "../sources/Classical-Dynamics-David-Tong.lingua#r_2M330E943Q98XH7BJKXETRWKNA", #source "../sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-MIT-Scholar.lingua#r_3VWHWXQT3WB39Y221AV2W9AE65", #mechanical-engineering "Generalized-coordinates-and-virtual-work.lingua#r_1EP507NTPSWGQ64SHWC5Z0YFHC") { r_4VJ79D0Q8KT7CC39Q1GRSDAAZ8
**Study:** [Engineering Dynamics — MIT 2.003SC, Fall 2011](../sources/Engineering-Dynamics-MIT-2-003SC.lingua#r_37MSK6EJHRV95GXWA23P4ZJPZ3)'s work–energy methods; [Classical Dynamics — David Tong](../sources/Classical-Dynamics-David-Tong.lingua#r_2M330E943Q98XH7BJKXETRWKNA), sections 1.2.3, 1.3.2 and 2.1. Use [Calculus and differential equations for dynamics — MIT Scholar courses](../sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-MIT-Scholar.lingua#r_3VWHWXQT3WB39Y221AV2W9AE65), line integrals and the chain rule, to derive the account.

Dot the particle equation m a = F with velocity and integrate in time: the net work ∫ F·dr equals the change in kinetic energy. For a rigid body, T = m v_G²/2 + ω·I_Gω/2. A conservative force can be written from a potential; its work changes that potential with the opposite sign. Frictional, damping and actuator forces require their own work terms.

Mechanical power is F·v at a translating application point and torque·angular velocity for the relevant rotational motion. A linear viscous damper opposing relative speed v dissipates c v², never negative for c ≥ 0. In a closed unforced spring–mass–damper model, d(T+V)/dt = −c v².

For a 4 kg slider moving at 3 m/s, T = 18 J. A linear spring of stiffness 800 N/m compressed by 0.15 m stores 9 J. With no losses and the remaining 9 J retained kinetically, the slider speed is about 2.1213 m/s. Check that the specified geometry and contacts actually permit this energy exchange.

**Work:** Derive the rolling-cylinder acceleration by energy, then compare with the force solution. Construct a machine-stroke account with acceleration, load work, braking and losses. Show why energy alone may not determine reactions, peak contact loads or a trajectory when several coordinates remain unknown.

